  "blogs": [
    {
      "title": "Deploying Sub-50ms Speculative Decoding in High-Concurrency Production Environments",
      "slug": "speculative-decoding-production",
      "excerpt": "An architectural breakdown of draft-model calibration, paged KV-cache pinning on sparse clusters, and eliminating memory bandwidth bottlenecks under 10k simultaneous inference requests.",
      "content": "# Deploying Sub-50ms Speculative Decoding in High-Concurrency Production Environments\n\nStandard Large Language Model inference remains notoriously memory-bandwidth bound. In token-by-token autoregressive decoding, every newly minted token forces high-bandwidth memory (HBM3e) to stream all model weights through compute cores for a single vector-matrix operation.\n\nSpeculative decoding breaks this limitation by decoupling draft generation from full verification. A compact, sovereign draft model guesses K sequential tokens ahead. The large target model then evaluates the draft sequence in a single forward pass—converting K memory-bound operations into one highly parallel compute-bound matrix multiplication.\n\n### Architectural Takeaways\nSpeculative speedup is fundamentally determined by the ratio of Target Forward Compute Time to Draft Token Time. Maintaining asymmetric draft-to-target parameter ratios between 1:8 and 1:12 remains the optimal operational envelope.",
      "coverImage": "https://lh3.googleusercontent.com/aida-public/AB6AXuAXmSZ94v_M04GhLcnLoA_K4Ncag4bbtM-AFxUrPHFmxEzeznXNMwPZCOnR7MiZ-gj9FpwT9f8cXyG1ZcUxYS1OMPL0E8BEjDSCCG57gfglehilPrK03-LG6Rrxw0D-rq7jqlUErdSe0Si_MOK1ijyPukXVrBE8OZgX5ENOFPop5cMmJ07foicV7nMCwDzlOxkv1zNEpB0LOE7qLP1mSfpfps3JDzJB8Ps68iX2bbaPHSYUHvviN18",
